[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The inter-office network path is healthy; the failure is at the application layer on the server side. From the affected Office A host (pc1, 10.1.0.100) we can reach the Office B server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) with 0% loss and ~1.8 ms RTT (pc1→10.5.0.4 ping succeeded). This excludes a Layer2/Layer3 link or routing failure between the buildings. However, srv1 is not providing any end-user service except DNS. Netstat on srv1 shows only TCP listeners on 10.5.0.4:53 and 127.0.0.1:53/953; there is no HTTP/HTTPS/SMB or other typical application port open. A test from pc1 to http://10.5.0.4 completes immediately, consistent with a connection refusal due to no web service listening. 

Mechanism: Users perceive “can’t reach the server” because their application connections are reset/refused at srv1; ICMP still works, so ping succeeds but the app fails. Localization: srv1 (Office B), eth0 10.5.0.4, server processes—only DNS is active; expected payroll application service is not running or not bound to 10.5.0.4. 

Root cause: The application service on srv1 was stopped/disabled or misconfigured during/after the “last night’s change,” leaving only DNS active.

Corrective fix: On srv1, start/enable the intended payroll service and ensure it listens on the correct port(s) and interface (10.5.0.4). If it’s web-based, start the web server (e.g., nginx/apache) or restore its config; if it’s another protocol (e.g., SMB), start that daemon and open the corresponding ports. No network changes are required; the inter-building link is up. 
=== END ===